@node	5.4, 5.5, 5.3, Chapter 5
@section The Explicit-Control Evaluator

In @ref{5.1} we saw how to transform simple programs into descriptions of
register machines.  We will now perform this transformation on a more complex
program, the metacircular evaluator of @ref{4.1.1}--@ref{4.1.4}, which shows
how the behavior of an interpreter for the OCaml subset can be described in
terms of the procedures @code{eval} and @code{apply}.  The
@newterm{explicit-control evaluator} that we develop in this section shows how
the underlying procedure-calling and argument-passing mechanisms used in the
evaluation process can be described in terms of operations on registers and
stacks.  In addition, the explicit-control evaluator can serve as an
implementation of an interpreter for the subset, written in a language that is
very similar to the native machine language of conventional computers.  The
evaluator can be executed by the register-machine simulator of @ref{5.2}.
Alternatively, it can be used as a starting point for building a
machine-language implementation of an evaluator, or even a special-purpose
machine for evaluating expressions.  @ref{Figure 5.16} shows such a hardware
implementation: a silicon chip that acts as an evaluator for a Lisp dialect.
The chip designers started with the data-path and controller specifications
for a register machine similar to the evaluator described in this section and
used design automation programs to construct the integrated-circuit
layout.@footnote{See @ref{Batali et al. 1982} for more information on the chip
and the method by which it was designed.}

