In this edition the explicit-control evaluator is the module
@code{Sec_5_4}.  It has three parts: the machine words that the registers
hold, a controller that is a list of the instructions of @ref{5.1.5}, and a
table of machine operations over the words.  The controller is written as
named fragments, one for each kind of expression, and the machine of
@ref{5.2} assembles their concatenation.  The controller listings of this
section are excerpts of those fragments, so they are the text that the
edition actually executes.  The fragments use a few short names for the
instructions:

@example
let r name = M.Reg name
let l name = M.Label_ref name
let assign target source = M.Assign (target, source)
let compute target op sources = M.Assign_op (target, op, sources)
let test op sources = M.Test (op, sources)
let branch_if op sources target =
  [ test op sources; M.Branch target ]
let return = M.Goto_reg "continue"
let dispatch = M.Goto "eval-dispatch"
@end example

@noindent
Thus @code{compute "val" "literal-value" [ r "exp" ]} is the book's
@code{(assign val (op literal-value) (reg exp))}, @code{return} is
@code{(goto (reg continue))}, and @code{dispatch} is
@code{(goto (label eval-dispatch))}.

@subsubheading Registers and operations

In designing the explicit-control evaluator, we must specify the operations to
be used in our register machine.  We described the metacircular evaluator in
terms of abstract syntax, using the typed view of expressions and procedures
such as @code{make_procedure}.  In implementing the register machine, we could
expand these procedures into sequences of elementary list-structure memory
operations, and implement these operations on our register machine.  However,
this would make our evaluator very long, obscuring the basic structure with
details.  To clarify the presentation, we will include as primitive operations
of the register machine the syntax tests and selectors of @ref{4.1.2} and the
procedures for representing environments and other run-time data given in
sections @ref{4.1.3} and @ref{4.1.4}.  In order to completely specify an
evaluator that could be programmed in a low-level machine language or
implemented in hardware, we would replace these operations by more elementary
operations, using the list-structure implementation we described in
@ref{5.3}.

The operations of this edition keep one rule: they inspect syntax, build
values, and bind environments, but they never evaluate a subexpression.
Every evaluation decision is an instruction of the controller.  Evaluation
order, tail calls, and stack use are therefore facts about the controller,
and the exercises of this section observe and change them.  The operations
reuse the operator semantics and the pattern matcher of @ref{4.1}, so the
machine computes what the evaluators of @code{Sec_4_1} compute.

Our evaluator register machine includes a stack and seven registers:
@code{exp}, @code{env}, @code{val}, @code{continue}, @code{proc}, @code{argl},
and @code{unev}.  @code{Exp} is used to hold the expression to be evaluated,
and @code{env} contains the environment in which the evaluation is to be
performed.  At the end of an evaluation, @code{val} contains the value obtained
by evaluating the expression in the designated environment.  The
@code{continue} register is used to implement recursion, as explained in
@ref{5.1.4}.  (The evaluator needs to call itself recursively, since
evaluating an expression requires evaluating its subexpressions.)  The
registers @code{proc}, @code{argl}, and @code{unev} are used in evaluating
applications, and the edition also uses them for constructions, @code{match},
and @code{let}.

A machine word is what a register holds, and the evaluator's registers
carry more than guest values.  @code{Exp} holds a typed expression from
@code{Check}, @code{unev} holds pending operands, the cases of a
@code{match}, or a pending recursive group, @code{argl} holds evaluated
operands in evaluation order, @code{env} holds an environment, and
@code{continue} holds a label.  A @code{V} word wraps a guest value:

@example ocaml
type word =
  | V of Value.t
  | Exp of Ast.expr
  | Exps of Ast.expr list
  | Args of Value.t list
  | Env of Env.t
  | Lab of string
  | Cases of (Ast.pattern * Ast.expr) list
  | Pat of Ast.pattern
  | Pending of Env.t * (Ast.binding * Env.cell option) list
  | Unassigned
@end example

We will not provide a data-path diagram to show how the registers and
operations of the evaluator are connected, nor will we give the complete list
of machine operations.  These are implicit in the evaluator's controller, which
will be presented in detail.  (The module lists them as
@code{base_operation_names}.)

