@subsection The Core of the Explicit-Control Evaluator

The central element in the evaluator is the sequence of instructions beginning
at @code{eval-dispatch}.  This corresponds to the @code{eval} procedure of the
metacircular evaluator described in @ref{4.1.1}.  When the controller
starts at @code{eval-dispatch}, it evaluates the expression specified by
@code{exp} in the environment specified by @code{env}.  When evaluation is
complete, the controller will go to the entry point stored in @code{continue},
and the @code{val} register will hold the value of the expression.  As with the
metacircular @code{eval}, the structure of @code{eval-dispatch} is a case
analysis on the syntactic type of the expression to be evaluated.@footnote{In
our controller, the dispatch is written as a sequence of @code{test} and
@code{branch} instructions.  Alternatively, it could have been written in a
data-directed style (and in a real system it probably would have been) to
avoid the need to perform sequential tests and to simplify the definition of
new expression types.  A machine designed to run such a language would
probably include a @code{dispatch-on-type} instruction that would efficiently
execute such data-directed dispatches.}

@example
let eval_dispatch =
  List.concat
    [ [ M.Label "eval-dispatch" ]
    ; branch_if "self-evaluating?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-self-eval"
    ; branch_if "variable?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-variable"
    ; branch_if "fun?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-lambda"
    ; branch_if "if?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-if"
    ; branch_if "match?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-match"
    ; branch_if "let-rec?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-let-rec"
    ; branch_if "let?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-let"
    ; branch_if "sequence?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-sequence"
    ; branch_if "and?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-and"
    ; branch_if "or?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-or"
    ; branch_if "unary?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-unary"
    ; branch_if "binary?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-binary"
    ; branch_if "construction?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-collect"
    ; branch_if "application?" [ r "exp" ] "ev-application"
    ; [ M.Perform ("unknown-expression-type", [ r "exp" ]) ]
    ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
The cases follow the syntax of the subset, not the book's list.  There
is no quotation.  An assignment is the operator @code{:=} on a reference,
so it is one of the binary operators.  A definition is a @code{let}, either
an expression or a top-level item that the driver of @ref{5.4.4} runs.  The
book's @code{begin} is the sequence @code{e1; e2}.  In return, the subset
has @code{match}, the short-circuit operators @code{&&} and @code{||}, the
operators, and the constructions (tuples, constructors, and records).

@subsubheading Evaluating simple expressions

Literals (which are self-evaluating), variables, and @code{fun} expressions
have no subexpressions to be evaluated.  For these, the evaluator simply
places the correct value in the @code{val} register and continues execution at
the entry point specified by @code{continue}.  Evaluation of simple
expressions is performed by the following controller code:

@example
let ev_simple =
  [ M.Label "ev-self-eval"
  ; compute "val" "literal-value" [ r "exp" ]
  ; return
  ; M.Label "ev-variable"
  ; compute "val" "lookup-variable-value" [ r "exp"; r "env" ]
  ; return
  ; M.Label "ev-lambda"
  ; compute "val" "make-procedure" [ r "exp"; r "env" ]
  ; return
  ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
The book's @code{ev-lambda} uses the @code{unev} and @code{exp} registers to
hold the parameters and body of the lambda expression so that they can be
passed to the @code{make-procedure} operation, along with the environment in
@code{env}.  Here @code{make-procedure} receives the whole @code{fun}
expression and the environment, and it takes the parameters and the body out
of the expression itself:

@example ocaml
; value_op "make-procedure" (function
    | [ e; env ] ->
      let* e = expr "make-procedure" e in
      let* env = environment "make-procedure" env in
      (match Ast.view e with
       | Ast.Fun (parameters, body) ->
         Ok (V (Value.closure ~name:None ~parameters ~body ~env))
       | _ -> shape "make-procedure")
    | ws -> arity 2 ws)
@end example

@subsubheading Evaluating procedure applications

A procedure application is specified by a combination containing an operator
and operands.  The operator is a subexpression whose value is a procedure, and
the operands are subexpressions whose values are the arguments to which the
procedure should be applied.  The metacircular @code{eval} handles applications
by calling itself recursively to evaluate each element of the combination, and
then passing the results to @code{apply}, which performs the actual procedure
application.  The explicit-control evaluator does the same thing; these
recursive calls are implemented by @code{goto} instructions, together with use
of the stack to save registers that will be restored after the recursive call
returns.  Before each call we will be careful to identify which registers must
be saved (because their values will be needed later).@footnote{This is an
important but subtle point in translating algorithms from a procedural
language, such as OCaml, to a register-machine language.  As an alternative to
saving only what is needed, we could save all the registers (except @code{val})
before each recursive call.  This is called a @newterm{framed-stack}
discipline.  This would work but might save more registers than necessary;
this could be an important consideration in a system where stack operations
are expensive.  Saving registers whose contents will not be needed later may
also hold onto useless data that could otherwise be garbage-collected, freeing
space to be reused.}

We begin the evaluation of an application by evaluating the operator to produce
a procedure, which will later be applied to the evaluated operands.  To
evaluate the operator, we move it to the @code{exp} register and go to
@code{eval-dispatch}.  The environment in the @code{env} register is already
the correct one in which to evaluate the operator.  However, we save @code{env}
because we will need it later to evaluate the operands.  We also extract the
operands into @code{unev} and save this on the stack.  We set up
@code{continue} so that @code{eval-dispatch} will resume at
@code{ev-appl-did-operator} after the operator has been evaluated.  First,
however, we save the old value of @code{continue}, which tells the controller
where to continue after the application.

@example ocaml
[ M.Label "ev-application"
; M.Save "continue"
; M.Save "env"
; compute "unev" "operands" [ r "exp" ]
; M.Save "unev"
; compute "exp" "operator" [ r "exp" ]
; assign "continue" (l "ev-appl-did-operator")
; dispatch
@end example

@noindent
Upon returning from evaluating the operator subexpression, we proceed to
evaluate the operands of the combination and to accumulate the resulting
arguments in a list, held in @code{argl}.  First we restore the unevaluated
operands and the environment.  We initialize @code{argl} to an empty list.
Then we assign to the @code{proc} register the procedure that was produced by
evaluating the operator.  If there are no operands, we go directly to
@code{apply-dispatch}.  Otherwise we save @code{proc} on the stack and start
the argument-evaluation loop:@footnote{The empty argument list is the constant
word @code{Args []}, and two operations of the table manipulate argument lists
and operand lists: @code{adjoin-arg} adds a value at the end of the argument
list, and @code{last-operand?} tests for the last operand in a combination:

@example ocaml
; test_op "last-operand?" (function
    | [ Exps es ] -> Ok (List.length es = 1)
    | ws -> arity 1 ws)
@end example

@example ocaml
; value_op "adjoin-arg" (function
    | [ v; vs ] ->
      let* v = value "adjoin-arg" v in
      let* vs = arguments "adjoin-arg" vs in
      Ok (Args (vs @@ [ v ]))
    | ws -> arity 2 ws)
@end example
}

@example ocaml
M.Label "ev-appl-did-operator"
; M.Restore "unev"
; M.Restore "env"
; assign "argl" (M.Const (Args []))
; assign "proc" (r "val")
; test "no-operands?" [ r "unev" ]
; M.Branch "apply-dispatch"
; M.Save "proc"
@end example

@noindent
Each cycle of the argument-evaluation loop evaluates an operand from the list
in @code{unev} and accumulates the result into @code{argl}.  To evaluate an
operand, we place it in the @code{exp} register and go to @code{eval-dispatch},
after setting @code{continue} so that execution will resume with the
argument-accumulation phase.  But first we save the arguments accumulated so
far (held in @code{argl}), the environment (held in @code{env}), and the
remaining operands to be evaluated (held in @code{unev}).  A special case is
made for the evaluation of the last operand, which is handled at
@code{ev-appl-last-arg}.

@example ocaml
M.Label "ev-appl-operand-loop"
; M.Save "argl"
; compute "exp" "first-operand" [ r "unev" ]
; test "last-operand?" [ r "unev" ]
; M.Branch "ev-appl-last-arg"
; M.Save "env"
; M.Save "unev"
; assign "continue" (l "ev-appl-accumulate-arg")
; dispatch
@end example

@noindent
When an operand has been evaluated, the value is accumulated into the list held
in @code{argl}.  The operand is then removed from the list of unevaluated
operands in @code{unev}, and the argument-evaluation continues.

@example ocaml
M.Label "ev-appl-accumulate-arg"
; M.Restore "unev"
; M.Restore "env"
; M.Restore "argl"
; compute "argl" "adjoin-arg" [ r "val"; r "argl" ]
; compute "unev" "rest-operands" [ r "unev" ]
; M.Goto "ev-appl-operand-loop"
@end example

@noindent
Evaluation of the last argument is handled differently.  There is no need to
save the environment or the list of unevaluated operands before going to
@code{eval-dispatch}, since they will not be required after the last operand is
evaluated.  Thus, we return from the evaluation to a special entry point
@code{ev-appl-accum-last-arg}, which restores the argument list, accumulates
the new argument, restores the saved procedure, and goes off to perform the
application.@footnote{The optimization of treating the last operand specially
is known as @newterm{evlis tail recursion} (see @ref{Wand 1980}).  We could be
somewhat more efficient in the argument evaluation loop if we made evaluation
of the first operand a special case too.  This would permit us to postpone
initializing @code{argl} until after evaluating the first operand, so as to
avoid saving @code{argl} in this case.  The compiler in @ref{5.5}
performs this optimization.  (Compare the @code{construct-arglist} procedure
of @ref{5.5.3}.)}

@example ocaml
M.Label "ev-appl-last-arg"
; assign "continue" (l "ev-appl-accum-last-arg")
; dispatch
; M.Label "ev-appl-accum-last-arg"
; M.Restore "argl"
; compute "argl" "adjoin-arg" [ r "val"; r "argl" ]
; M.Restore "proc"
; M.Goto "apply-dispatch"
@end example

@noindent
The details of the argument-evaluation loop determine the order in which the
interpreter evaluates the operands of a combination (e.g., left to right or
right to left; see @ref{Exercise 3.8}).  Native OCaml leaves this order
unspecified, so it is not something an evaluator can inherit from its host.
The metacircular evaluator fixes it by the recursion it writes
out.@footnote{The order of operand evaluation in the metacircular evaluator is
determined by the recursion in @code{eval_list} of @ref{4.1.1} (see
@ref{Exercise 4.1}).}  Because the @code{first-operand} operation (used in
@code{ev-appl-operand-loop}) takes the head of the list in @code{unev} and the
@code{rest-operands} operation takes its tail, the explicit-control evaluator
will evaluate the operands of a combination in left-to-right order.

@subsubheading Procedure application

The entry point @code{apply-dispatch} corresponds to the @code{apply} procedure
of the metacircular evaluator.  By the time we get to @code{apply-dispatch},
the @code{proc} register contains the procedure to apply and @code{argl}
contains the list of evaluated arguments to which it must be applied.  The
saved value of @code{continue} (originally passed to @code{eval-dispatch} and
saved at @code{ev-application}), which tells where to return with the result of
the procedure application, is on the stack.  When the application is complete,
the controller transfers to the entry point specified by the saved
@code{continue}, with the result of the application in @code{val}.  As with the
metacircular @code{apply}, there are two cases to consider.  Either the
procedure to be applied is a primitive or it is a compound procedure.

@example ocaml
M.Label "apply-entry"
; M.Save "continue"
; M.Label "apply-dispatch"
; test "primitive-procedure?" [ r "proc" ]
; M.Branch "primitive-apply"
; test "compound-procedure?" [ r "proc" ]
; M.Branch "compound-apply"
; M.Perform ("signal-not-applicable", [ r "proc" ])
@end example

@noindent
The extra entry @code{apply-entry} saves @code{continue} itself.  It serves a
primitive such as @code{List.map} that calls a guest procedure: the primitive
runs the guest procedure on a fresh machine with the same controller, entered
at @code{apply-entry}.

Guest procedures are curried, and this makes application in the subset
different from the book's in two ways.  A procedure may receive fewer
arguments than it has parameters, and then the answer is a new procedure
that waits for the rest.  A procedure may also receive more arguments than it
has parameters, and then its value must be a procedure, which is applied to
the arguments left over.  In the program

@example ocaml
let add x y = x + y
let inc = add 1
let pick b = if b then add else fun x y -> x - y
let () = print_int (inc 41); print_newline ()
let () = print_int (pick false 10 3)
@end example

@noindent
@code{add 1} is a partial application, and @code{pick false 10 3} gives
@code{pick} two arguments more than its one parameter.  The program prints
@code{42} and @code{7}.

We assume that each primitive is implemented so as to obtain its arguments from
@code{argl} and place its result in @code{val}.  To specify how the machine
handles primitives, we would have to provide a sequence of controller
instructions to implement each primitive and arrange for
@code{primitive-apply} to dispatch to the instructions for the primitive
identified by the contents of @code{proc}.  Since we are interested in the
structure of the evaluation process rather than the details of the primitives,
we will instead just use an @code{apply-primitive-procedure} operation that
applies the procedure in @code{proc} to the arguments in @code{argl}.  For the
purpose of simulating the evaluator with the simulator of @ref{5.2} we use
the primitives of @ref{4.1.4}, which call on the host OCaml system to perform
the application.  A primitive takes only the arguments it needs; if there are
too few, @code{apply-primitive-procedure} answers a partial primitive, and
@code{primitive-excess-arguments} answers the arguments left over.  After
computing the value of the primitive application, we restore @code{continue}
and go to @code{apply-excess}, which applies the value to any arguments left
over.

@example ocaml
M.Label "primitive-apply"
; compute "val" "apply-primitive-procedure" [ r "proc"; r "argl" ]
; compute "argl" "primitive-excess-arguments" [ r "proc"; r "argl" ]
; M.Restore "continue"
; M.Goto "apply-excess"
@end example

@noindent
To apply a compound procedure, we proceed just as with the metacircular
evaluator.  We construct a frame that binds the procedure's parameters to the
arguments, use this frame to extend the environment carried by the procedure,
and evaluate in this extended environment the expression that forms the body of
the procedure.  @code{Bind-parameters} binds as many arguments as there are
parameters, and @code{compound-excess-arguments} answers the rest.  If no
arguments are left over, the body is evaluated at @code{compound-tail}, after
restoring @code{continue}, so that it returns directly to the caller of the
application; @ref{5.4.2} explains why this matters.  Otherwise the body is
evaluated as a subproblem, with the extra arguments saved, and its value is
then applied to them.

@example ocaml
M.Label "compound-apply"
; test "partial-application?" [ r "proc"; r "argl" ]
; M.Branch "compound-partial"
; compute "env" "bind-parameters" [ r "proc"; r "argl" ]
; compute "exp" "procedure-body" [ r "proc" ]
; compute "argl" "compound-excess-arguments" [ r "proc"; r "argl" ]
; test "no-arguments?" [ r "argl" ]
; M.Branch "compound-tail"
; M.Save "argl"
; assign "continue" (l "compound-excess")
; dispatch
; M.Label "compound-excess"
; M.Restore "argl"
; M.Restore "continue"
; M.Goto "apply-excess"
; M.Label "compound-tail"
; M.Restore "continue"
; dispatch
@end example

@noindent
A procedure given too few arguments answers the partial procedure, whose
environment binds the parameters that received arguments.  At
@code{apply-excess}, a value with no arguments left over is the value of the
application, and otherwise the value becomes the procedure of a new
application to the arguments left over:

@example ocaml
M.Label "compound-partial"
; compute "val" "partial-procedure" [ r "proc"; r "argl" ]
; M.Restore "continue"
; return
; M.Label "apply-excess"
; test "no-arguments?" [ r "argl" ]
; M.Branch "ev-return-val"
; assign "proc" (r "val")
; M.Save "continue"
; M.Goto "apply-dispatch"
@end example

@noindent
@code{Bind-parameters} is the only place in the interpreter where the
@code{env} register receives the environment of a procedure.  The other
assignments to @code{env}, in @code{ev-match} and @code{ev-let} of
@ref{5.4.3}, extend the current environment with the names that a pattern or
a @code{let} binds.  Just as in the metacircular evaluator, the new environment
is constructed from the environment carried by the procedure, together with the
argument list and the corresponding list of variables to be bound:

@example ocaml
; value_op "bind-parameters" (function
    | [ proc; vs ] ->
      let* c = closure "bind-parameters" proc in
      let* vs = arguments "bind-parameters" vs in
      let n = List.length c.parameters in
      Ok
        (Env
           (Env.extend (List.combine c.parameters (take n vs)) c.env))
    | ws -> arity 2 ws)
@end example

@subsubheading Evaluating operators and constructions

The subset's operators are expressions of their own, not applications of
primitive procedures.  A unary operator (@code{not}, negation, @code{!},
@code{ref}, and field access) evaluates its operand with @code{exp} and
@code{continue} saved and then applies the operator to the value:

@example
let ev_unary =
  [ M.Label "ev-unary"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-unary-apply")
  ; compute "exp" "unary-operand" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-unary-apply"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; compute "val" "apply-unary" [ r "exp"; r "val" ]
  ; return
  ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
A binary operator (arithmetic, comparison, @code{^}, @code{::}, and
@code{:=}) evaluates the left operand, saves its value, evaluates the right
operand, and restores the left value into @code{argl}:

@example
let ev_binary =
  [ M.Label "ev-binary"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "env"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-binary-right")
  ; compute "exp" "left-operand" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-binary-right"
  ; M.Restore "env"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "val"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-binary-apply")
  ; compute "exp" "right-operand" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-binary-apply"
  ; M.Restore "argl"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; compute "val" "apply-binary" [ r "exp"; r "argl"; r "val" ]
  ; return
  ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
Tuples, constructor applications, and records evaluate a list of operands from
left to right into @code{argl}, with the same loop as the argument loop of an
application.  The parallel @code{let} of @ref{5.4.3} uses the loop for its
right-hand sides.  When the loop is done, the expression in @code{exp} decides
what the list builds:

@example
let ev_collect =
  [ M.Label "ev-collect"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; assign "argl" (M.Const (Args []))
  ; compute "unev" "collected-operands" [ r "exp" ]
  ; M.Label "ev-collect-loop"
  ; test "no-operands?" [ r "unev" ]
  ; M.Branch "ev-collect-done"
  ; M.Save "argl"
  ; M.Save "env"
  ; M.Save "unev"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-collect-accumulate")
  ; compute "exp" "first-operand" [ r "unev" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-collect-accumulate"
  ; M.Restore "unev"
  ; M.Restore "env"
  ; M.Restore "argl"
  ; compute "argl" "adjoin-arg" [ r "val"; r "argl" ]
  ; compute "unev" "rest-operands" [ r "unev" ]
  ; M.Goto "ev-collect-loop"
  ; M.Label "ev-collect-done"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; test "let?" [ r "exp" ]
  ; M.Branch "ev-let-bind"
  ; compute "val" "build" [ r "exp"; r "argl" ]
  ; return
  ]
;;
@end example

@node	5.4.2, 5.4.3, 5.4.1, 5.4
@subsection Sequence Evaluation and Tail Recursion

The portion of the explicit-control evaluator at @code{ev-sequence} is
analogous to the metacircular evaluator's treatment of sequences.  It handles
the sequence expression @code{e1; e2} of the subset, which takes the place of
the book's @code{begin}.  A sequence has exactly two parts; a longer sequence
@code{e1; e2; e3} nests to the right, as @code{e1; (e2; e3)}.  The
book's second source of sequences, the several expressions of a procedure
body, does not exist in the subset: a body is a single expression.

The evaluator saves @code{exp}, @code{env}, and @code{continue}, and evaluates
the first expression with @code{continue} set to @code{ev-sequence-rest}.  When
the first expression has been evaluated, the saved registers are restored and
the second expression is evaluated.

The second expression is handled differently from the first.  Since there are
no more expressions to be evaluated after this one, we need not save anything
before going to @code{eval-dispatch}.  The value of the whole sequence is the
value of the second expression, so after its evaluation there is nothing left
to do except continue at the entry point that @code{continue} held when the
sequence began.  Rather than setting up @code{continue} to arrange for
@code{eval-dispatch} to return here and then restoring @code{continue} from
the stack and continuing at that entry point, we restore @code{continue}
before going to @code{eval-dispatch}, so that @code{eval-dispatch} will
continue at that entry point after evaluating the expression.

@example
let ev_sequence =
  [ M.Label "ev-sequence"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "env"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-sequence-rest")
  ; compute "exp" "first-expression" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-sequence-rest"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; M.Restore "env"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; compute "exp" "second-expression" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ]
;;
@end example

@subsubheading Tail recursion

In @ref{Chapter 1} we said that the process described by a procedure such as

@example
let rec sqrt_iter guess x =
  if good_enough guess x then guess
  else sqrt_iter (improve guess x) x
@end example

@noindent
is an iterative process.  Even though the procedure is syntactically recursive
(defined in terms of itself), it is not logically necessary for an evaluator to
save information in passing from one call to @code{sqrt_iter} to the
next.@footnote{We saw in @ref{5.1} how to implement such a process with a
register machine that had no stack; the state of the process was stored in a
fixed set of registers.}  An evaluator that can execute a procedure such as
@code{sqrt_iter} without requiring increasing storage as the procedure
continues to call itself is called a @newterm{tail-recursive} evaluator.  The
metacircular implementation of the evaluator in @ref{Chapter 4} does not
specify whether the evaluator is tail-recursive, because that evaluator
inherits its mechanism for saving state from the host OCaml.  With the
explicit-control evaluator, however, we can trace through the evaluation
process to see when procedure calls cause a net accumulation of information on
the stack.

Our evaluator is tail-recursive, because every expression in tail position is
evaluated by a transfer directly to @code{eval-dispatch} without saving any
information on the stack.  The second expression of a sequence is one such
place.  The others are the branches of an @code{if}, the body of the selected
@code{match} case, the body of a @code{let}, the right operand of @code{&&} and
@code{||}, and the body of a procedure at @code{compound-tail}.  Hence,
evaluating the body of @code{sqrt_iter}, whose @code{if} reduces to a call to
@code{sqrt_iter}, will not cause any information to be accumulated on the
stack.@footnote{This implementation of tail recursion is one variety of a
well-known optimization technique used by many compilers.  In compiling a
procedure that ends with a procedure call, one can replace the call by a jump
to the called procedure's entry point.  Building this strategy into the
interpreter, as we have done in this section, provides the optimization
uniformly throughout the language.}

If we did not think to take advantage of the fact that it was unnecessary to
save information in this case, we might have implemented @code{ev-sequence} by
treating both expressions of a sequence in the same way, saving the registers,
evaluating the expression, returning to restore the registers, and only then
returning to the caller:@footnote{The naive version needs no new operation.
It keeps @code{first-expression} and @code{second-expression}, and it adds
only the entry @code{ev-sequence-last-done}, to which the evaluation of the
second expression returns.}

@example
let naive_ev_sequence =
  [ M.Label "ev-sequence"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "env"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; M.Assign ("continue", M.Label_ref "ev-sequence-rest")
  ; M.Assign_op ("exp", "first-expression", [ r "exp" ])
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-sequence-rest"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; M.Restore "env"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; M.Assign_op ("exp", "second-expression", [ r "exp" ])
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; M.Assign ("continue", M.Label_ref "ev-sequence-last-done")
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-sequence-last-done"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; M.Goto_reg "continue"
  ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
This may seem like a minor change to our previous code for evaluation of a
sequence: The only difference is that we go through the save-restore cycle
for the last expression in a sequence as well as for the others.  The
interpreter will still give the same value for any expression.  But this
change is fatal to the tail-recursive implementation, because we must now
return after evaluating the final expression in a sequence in order to undo
the (useless) register saves.  These extra saves will accumulate during a nest
of procedure calls.  Consequently, processes such as @code{sqrt_iter} will
require space proportional to the number of iterations rather than requiring
constant space.  This difference can be significant.  For example, with tail
recursion, an infinite loop can be expressed using only the procedure-call
mechanism:

@example
let rec count n =
  print_int n;
  print_newline ();
  count (n + 1)
@end example

@noindent
Without tail recursion, such a procedure would eventually run out of stack
space, and expressing a true iteration would require some control mechanism
other than procedure call.

@node	5.4.3, 5.4.4, 5.4.2, 5.4
@subsection Conditionals, Assignments, and Definitions

As with the metacircular evaluator, special forms are handled by selectively
evaluating fragments of the expression.  For an @code{if} expression, we must
evaluate the predicate and decide, based on the value of predicate, whether to
evaluate the consequent or the alternative.

Before evaluating the predicate, we save the @code{if} expression itself so
that we can later extract the consequent or alternative.  We also save the
environment, which we will need later to evaluate the consequent or the
alternative, and we save @code{continue}, which we will need later in order to
return to the evaluation of the expression that is waiting for the value of the
@code{if}.

When we return from evaluating the predicate, we test whether it was true or
false and, depending on the result, place either the consequent or the
alternative in @code{exp} before going to @code{eval-dispatch}.  Notice that
restoring @code{env} and @code{continue} here sets up @code{eval-dispatch} to
have the correct environment and to continue at the right place to receive the
value of the @code{if} expression.

@example
let ev_if =
  [ M.Label "ev-if"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "env"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-if-decide")
  ; compute "exp" "if-predicate" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-if-decide"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; M.Restore "env"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; test "true?" [ r "val" ]
  ; M.Branch "ev-if-consequent"
  ; compute "exp" "if-alternative" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-if-consequent"
  ; compute "exp" "if-consequent" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
The test @code{true?} accepts only a @code{bool}.  Admission has already
type-checked the program, so a condition is always a @code{bool}.

The short-circuit operators have the same shape.  @code{&&} evaluates its
left operand, and a @code{false} value decides the answer; otherwise the right
operand is evaluated in tail position.  @code{||} is the same with
@code{true}.  One fragment builds both entries:

@example
let ev_logic =
  let block name decided =
    [ M.Label ("ev-" ^ name)
    ; M.Save "exp"
    ; M.Save "env"
    ; M.Save "continue"
    ; assign "continue" (l ("ev-" ^ name ^ "-decide"))
    ; compute "exp" "left-operand" [ r "exp" ]
    ; dispatch
    ; M.Label ("ev-" ^ name ^ "-decide")
    ; M.Restore "continue"
    ; M.Restore "env"
    ; M.Restore "exp"
    ; test decided [ r "val" ]
    ; M.Branch "ev-return-val"
    ; compute "exp" "right-operand" [ r "exp" ]
    ; dispatch
    ]
  in
  List.concat
    [ block "and" "false?"
    ; block "or" "true?"
    ; [ M.Label "ev-return-val"; return ]
    ]
;;
@end example

@noindent
The multi-way conditional of the subset is @code{match}.  The evaluator
evaluates the scrutinee once, as it evaluates the predicate of an @code{if},
and then tries the cases in order.  @code{Try-first-case} runs the pattern
matcher of @ref{4.1} on the first case in @code{unev}.  A pattern that
matches answers the environment extended with the names it binds, and one that
does not answers the word @code{Unassigned}.  The body of the first case that
matches runs in that environment, in tail position:

@example
let ev_match =
  [ M.Label "ev-match"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "env"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-match-cases")
  ; compute "exp" "match-scrutinee" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-match-cases"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; M.Restore "env"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; compute "unev" "match-cases" [ r "exp" ]
  ; M.Label "ev-match-loop"
  ; test "no-cases?" [ r "unev" ]
  ; M.Branch "match-failure"
  ; compute "argl" "try-first-case" [ r "unev"; r "val"; r "env" ]
  ; test "matched?" [ r "argl" ]
  ; M.Branch "ev-match-body"
  ; compute "unev" "rest-cases" [ r "unev" ]
  ; M.Goto "ev-match-loop"
  ; M.Label "ev-match-body"
  ; assign "env" (r "argl")
  ; compute "exp" "first-case-body" [ r "unev" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "match-failure"
  ; M.Perform ("signal-match-failure", [ r "val" ])
  ]
;;
@end example

@example ocaml
; value_op "try-first-case" (function
    | [ Cases ((pattern, _) :: _); v; env ] ->
      let* v = value "try-first-case" v in
      let* env = environment "try-first-case" env in
      (match Sec_4_1.bind_pattern pattern v with
       | Some bindings -> Ok (Env (Env.extend bindings env))
       | None -> Ok Unassigned)
    | ws -> arity 3 ws)
; test_op "matched?" (function
    | [ Env _ ] -> Ok true
    | [ _ ] -> Ok false
    | ws -> arity 1 ws)
@end example

@subsubheading Assignments and definitions

An assignment @code{r := v} changes the contents of a reference cell, not a
binding.  It is a binary operator, so @code{ev-binary} of @ref{5.4.1}
evaluates the reference and then the new value, and @code{apply-binary} stores
the value in the cell.  The book's @code{set-variable-value!} has become one
case of the function that applies binary operators, and the value of the
assignment is the unit value @code{()}:

@example ocaml
| Ast.Assign _ ->
  (match Value.view left with
   | Value.Ref cell ->
     cell := right;
     Ok Value.unit
   | _ -> type_error ":= target is not a reference")
@end example

@noindent
Definitions are the @code{let} forms.  A parallel @code{let} evaluates its
right-hand sides with the collection loop of @code{ev-collect}, which returns
to @code{ev-let-bind}.  @code{Let-environment} then extends the environment
with the names and values, and the body is evaluated in tail position.  A
@code{let rec} group first extends the environment with a fresh, unfilled cell
for each name.  It then evaluates the right-hand sides in that environment, in
order, and fills each cell with its value, so each right-hand side can refer
to the names of the group.  The top-level @code{let} items of a program are
run by the driver of @ref{5.4.4} in the same way.

@example
let ev_let =
  [ M.Label "ev-let-rec"
  ; compute "unev" "let-rec-group" [ r "exp"; r "env" ]
  ; compute "env" "group-environment" [ r "unev" ]
  ; M.Label "ev-let-rec-loop"
  ; test "no-pending?" [ r "unev" ]
  ; M.Branch "ev-let-body"
  ; M.Save "continue"
  ; M.Save "exp"
  ; M.Save "env"
  ; M.Save "unev"
  ; assign "continue" (l "ev-let-rec-fill")
  ; compute "exp" "first-pending-rhs" [ r "unev" ]
  ; dispatch
  ; M.Label "ev-let-rec-fill"
  ; M.Restore "unev"
  ; M.Restore "env"
  ; M.Restore "exp"
  ; M.Restore "continue"
  ; M.Perform ("fill-first-pending", [ r "unev"; r "val" ])
  ; compute "unev" "rest-pending" [ r "unev" ]
  ; M.Goto "ev-let-rec-loop"
  ; M.Label "ev-let"
  ; M.Goto "ev-collect"
  ; M.Label "ev-let-bind"
  ; compute "env" "let-environment" [ r "exp"; r "argl"; r "env" ]
  ; M.Label "ev-let-body"
  ; compute "exp" "let-body" [ r "exp" ]
  ; dispatch
  ]
;;
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.23}Exercise 5.23:} The subset has no @code{cond}.
Its multi-way conditional is the literal @code{match}, a @code{match} whose
cases are literals, a variable, or the wildcard, as in
@code{match n with 0 -> "zero" | 1 -> "one" | _ -> "many"}.  Extend the
evaluator to handle derived expressions such as this @code{cond} and the
parallel @code{let} (@ref{4.1.2}) by transforming them, ahead of the base
@code{ev-match} and @code{ev-let}.  You may ``cheat'' and assume that syntax
transformers such as @code{cond->if} and @code{let->combination} are
available as machine operations.@footnote{This isn't really cheating.  In an
actual implementation built from scratch, we would use our explicit-control
evaluator to interpret a program that performs source-level transformations
like @code{cond->if} in a syntax phase that runs before execution.}
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.24}Exercise 5.24:} Implement the literal
@code{match} of @ref{Exercise 5.23} as a new basic special form, without
reducing it to @code{if} and without the general pattern matcher of
@code{try-first-case}.  You will have to construct a loop that compares the
value of the scrutinee with the literal of successive cases until you find a
case that selects it, and then evaluate the body of that case in tail
position.
@end quotation


@quotation
@emph{Exercise 5.24a is added by this edition and extends exercise
5.24; SICP numbers stop at 5.24.} @strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.24a}Exercise
5.24a:} In the same style, make chains of @code{&&} and of @code{||} basic
special forms of the controller.  A chain @code{a && b && c} nests to the
right in the syntax, and the base controller saves @code{continue} at each
node.  Each form needs a loop over its operand sequence that evaluates
operands until the chain's value is decided: @code{&&} stops at a
@code{false} operand, @code{||} at a @code{true} one, and the last operand of
each chain is evaluated in tail position.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.25}Exercise 5.25:} Modify the evaluator so that it
uses normal-order evaluation, based on the lazy evaluator of @ref{4.2}.
@end quotation

@node	5.4.4, 5.5, 5.3, 5.4
@subsection Running the Evaluator

With the implementation of the explicit-control evaluator we come to the end of
a development, begun in @ref{Chapter 1}, in which we have explored successively
more precise models of the evaluation process.  We started with the relatively
informal substitution model, then extended this in @ref{Chapter 3} to the
environment model, which enabled us to deal with state and change.  In the
metacircular evaluator of @ref{Chapter 4}, we used OCaml itself as a language
for making more explicit the environment structure constructed during
evaluation of an expression.  Now, with register machines, we have taken a
close look at the evaluator's mechanisms for storage management, argument
passing, and control.  At each new level of description, we have had to raise
issues and resolve ambiguities that were not apparent at the previous, less
precise treatment of evaluation.  To understand the behavior of the
explicit-control evaluator, we can simulate it and monitor its performance.

We will install a driver in our evaluator machine.  This plays the role of the
driver loop of @ref{4.1.4}.  The book's driver loop repeatedly prints a
prompt, reads an expression, evaluates the expression by going to
@code{eval-dispatch}, and prints the result.  In this edition the reading is
done before the machine runs: @code{Check.check} reads and type-checks the
whole program, and the driver then runs its top-level items in source order.
For each item, the driver function @code{enter} restarts the machine, sets the
registers, and starts the controller at @code{eval-dispatch}.  The controller
begins with the fragment @code{driver} and ends with the label
@code{done}:@footnote{We assume here that reading and the various printing
operations are available as primitive operations, which is useful for our
simulation, but completely unrealistic in practice.  These are actually
extremely complex operations.  In practice, they would be implemented using
low-level input-output operations such as transferring single characters to and
from a device.

The global environment is built when the evaluator is made:
@code{Prelude.initial_env} puts the primitives of @ref{4.1} into its
first frame, with printing directed to the function the driver provides.}

@example
let driver =
  [ assign "continue" (l "done"); dispatch ]
@end example

@example
let enter m label registers =
  M.restart m;
  let* () =
    List.fold_left
      (fun acc (name, w) ->
        let* () = acc in
        M.set_register m name w)
      (Ok ())
      (("continue", Lab "done") :: registers)
  in
  let* () = M.goto_label m label in
  collect m
;;
@end example

@example
let eval ev env e =
  enter ev.machine "eval-dispatch" [ "exp", Exp e; "env", Env env ]
@end example

@noindent
When we encounter an error in a procedure (such as the attempt to apply a
value that is not a procedure, indicated at @code{apply-dispatch}), the book
prints an error message and returns to the driver loop.@footnote{There are
other errors that we would like the interpreter to handle, but these are not so
simple.  See @ref{Exercise 5.30}.}  In this edition the operations
@code{unknown-expression-type} and @code{signal-not-applicable} answer an
error of type @code{Eval_error.t}, which stops the machine.  The driver then
answers that error for the program:

@example ocaml
; ( "unknown-expression-type"
  , M.Action_op (fun _ ->
      Error (Eval_error.Invalid_form "unknown expression type")) )
@end example

@example ocaml
; ( "signal-not-applicable"
  , M.Action_op
      (function
        | [ V v ] ->
          Error (Eval_error.Not_applicable (Value.to_string v))
        | ws -> arity 1 ws) )
@end example

@noindent
For the purposes of the simulation, we initialize the stack each time the
driver enters the machine (the call @code{M.restart} above), since it might
not be empty after an error (such as a division by zero) interrupts an
evaluation.@footnote{We could perform the stack initialization only after
errors, but doing it in the driver will be convenient for monitoring the
evaluator's performance, as described below.}

If we combine all the code fragments presented in
@ref{5.4.1}--@ref{5.4.4}, we can create an evaluator machine model that we can
run.  The fragments are listed in the book's order, and the base controller is
their concatenation:

@example
let controller_fragments =
  [ "driver", driver
  ; "eval-dispatch", eval_dispatch
  ; "ev-simple", ev_simple
  ; "ev-if", ev_if
  ; "ev-match", ev_match
  ; "ev-let", ev_let
  ; "ev-sequence", ev_sequence
  ; "ev-logic", ev_logic
  ; "ev-unary", ev_unary
  ; "ev-binary", ev_binary
  ; "ev-collect", ev_collect
  ; "ev-application", ev_application
  ; "apply-dispatch", apply_dispatch
  ; "done", [ M.Label "done" ]
  ]
;;

let base_controller = List.concat_map snd controller_fragments
@end example

@noindent
We must define host procedures to simulate the operations used as primitives
by the evaluator.  These are the same procedures we used for the metacircular
evaluator in @ref{4.1}, together with the few additional ones shown
throughout @ref{5.4}; @code{operation_table} collects them.
@code{Make_evaluator} assembles a controller with this table and the global
environment, and @code{run} runs a checked program on the base controller:

@example
let run ~emit program =
  let* ev = make_evaluator ~controller:base_controller ~emit () in
  run_program ev program
;;
@end example

@noindent
Here is a program for the evaluator, with the output it prints:

@example ocaml
let rec append x y =
  match x with
  | [] -> y
  | h :: t -> h :: append t y

let () = print_int (List.length (append [ 1; 2; 3 ] [ 4; 5; 6 ]))
(* prints 6 *)
@end example

@noindent
Of course, evaluating expressions in this way will take much longer than if we
had directly typed them into the host language, because of the levels of
simulation involved.  Our expressions are evaluated by the
explicit-control-evaluator machine, which is being simulated by the
register-machine simulator of @ref{5.2}, which is itself an OCaml program.  The
section replay pins the output above.

@subsubheading Monitoring the performance of the evaluator

Simulation can be a powerful tool to guide the implementation of evaluators.
Simulations make it easy not only to explore variations of the register-machine
design but also to monitor the performance of the simulated evaluator.  For
example, one important factor in performance is how efficiently the evaluator
uses the stack.  We can observe the number of stack operations required to
evaluate various expressions with the simulator's stack statistics
(@ref{5.2.4}).  The book adds an instruction at the evaluator's
@code{print-result} entry point to print them.  Here the function
@code{stack_statistics_after} runs a program and reads the statistics of its
last top-level evaluation:

@example
let stack_statistics_after program =
  let* ev =
    make_evaluator ~controller:base_controller ~emit:ignore ()
  in
  let* _ = run_program ev program in
  Ok (M.stack_statistics ev.machine)
;;
@end example

@noindent
For the recursive factorial, the definition item evaluates a @code{fun}
expression, which uses no stack (total pushes 0, maximum depth 0).  The call
gives these statistics:

@example ocaml
let rec factorial n = if n = 1 then 1 else factorial (n - 1) * n
let v = factorial 5
(* total-pushes = 105  maximum-depth = 18;  v = 120 *)
@end example

@noindent
Note that the driver reinitializes the stack at the start of each top-level
item, so that the statistics refer only to stack operations used to evaluate
that item.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.26}Exercise 5.26:} Use the monitored stack to
explore the tail-recursive property of the evaluator (@ref{5.4.2}).
Start the evaluator and define the iterative @code{factorial} procedure from
@ref{1.2.1}:

@example ocaml
let factorial n =
  let rec iter product counter =
    if counter > n then product
    else iter (counter * product) (counter + 1)
  in
  iter 1 1
@end example

Run the procedure with some small values of @math{n}.  Record the maximum stack
depth and the number of pushes required to compute @math{{n!}} for each of these
values.

@enumerate a

@item
You will find that the maximum depth required to evaluate @math{{n!}} is independent
of @math{n}.  What is that depth?

@item
Determine from your data a formula in terms of @math{n} for the total number of
push operations used in evaluating @math{{n!}} for any @math{{n \ge 1}}.  Note that the
number of operations used is a linear function of @math{n} and is thus determined
by two constants.

@end enumerate
