@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.30}Exercise 5.30:} Our evaluator currently catches
and signals only a few kinds of errors: unknown expression types, values that
are not procedures, and a @code{match} with no matching case.  Other errors
will take us out of the evaluator's driver.  When we run the evaluator using
the register-machine simulator, these errors are caught by the host: an
operation answers an @code{Eval_error.t}, and the machine stops.  This is
analogous to the computer crashing when a user program makes an
error.@footnote{Regrettably, this is the normal state of affairs in
conventional compiler-based language systems such as C.  In @abbr{UNIX}(tm)
the system ``dumps core,'' and in @abbr{DOS}/Windows(tm) it becomes
catatonic.  The Macintosh(tm) displays a picture of an exploding bomb and
offers you the opportunity to reboot the computer, if you're lucky.}  It is a
large project to make a real error system work, but it is well worth the effort
to understand what is involved here.

@enumerate a

@item
Errors that occur in the evaluation process, such as an attempt to access an
unbound variable, could be caught by changing the lookup operation to make it
return a distinguished condition code, which cannot be a possible value of any
user variable.  The evaluator can test for this condition code and then do what
is necessary to go to @code{signal-error}.  Find all of the places in the
evaluator where such a change is necessary and fix them.  This is lots of work.
(In the subset, admission rejects a program with an unbound variable or a
wrong number of operands before it runs.  Show that it does, and decide which
evaluation errors a checked program can still reach.)

@item
Much worse is the problem of handling errors that are signaled by applying
primitive procedures and operators, such as an attempt to divide by zero or an
attempt to read an array outside its bounds.  In a professionally written high-quality
system, each primitive application is checked for safety as part of the
primitive.  For example, every call to @code{Array.get} could first check that
the index is within the bounds of the array.  If it is not, the application
would return a distinguished condition code to the evaluator, which would then
report the failure.  We could arrange for this in our register-machine
simulator by making each primitive procedure check for applicability and
returning an appropriate distinguished condition code on failure.  Then the
@code{primitive-apply} and @code{ev-binary} code in the evaluator can check
for the condition code and go to @code{signal-error} if necessary.  Build this structure and make it
work.  This is a major project.

@end enumerate
@end quotation
